## 8. The image decoder

The image VAE keeps H3's encoder and latent space and swaps the decoder. H3's ViT decoder turns each latent voxel into a
4 × 16 × 16 pixel block, with the time axis spread over the clip:

{{ code(H3VAE, function='MiniMaxH3VideoViTDecoder3d.forward', root=DM) }}

P-H3-Image's decodes one frame. Each image-latent token becomes a 16 × 16 block, and every one of them sits at one
fixed time coordinate: the one H3's decoder gives the first frame of a 7-frame window. The register tokens and the
zero token appended after them sit at position 0 in both decoders, and are dropped before the pixels.

{{ code(PVAE, var='FRAME_POSITION', root=DM) }}
{{ code(PVAE, function='PH3ImageViTDecoder.forward', root=DM) }}

Ours adds both differences to H3's decoder as parameters, so the blocks, attention and tiling are shared. The fixed
coordinate replaces the time axis of the position grid; `_decoder` passes a temporal patch of 1 and the coordinate when
the VAE is single-frame:

{{ code('src/h3mlx/vae_ops.py', function='vit_positions') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/vae.py', var='IMAGE_FRAME_POSITION') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/vae.py', function='VideoVAE._decoder') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/vae.py', function='VideoVAE.decode') }}

`VaeConfig.from_diffusers` turns the mode on from the config's `_class_name`. The image VAE's `decode` and `encode`
refuse more than one frame, and ours does too.

**Precision.** With a `torch_dtype`, diffusers loads every parameter in that dtype except the class's
`_keep_in_fp32_modules`, matched as a component of the dotted name. The two VAE classes keep different lists:
`["encoder", "quant_conv", "proj_out"]` for the image VAE, and `encoder`, `quant_conv`, the three norms and the two
layer scales for H3's. The release stores the image decoder in bf16 (its `proj_out` included) and the encoder in float32
(read from its safetensors header), so loading in bf16 upcasts `proj_out` to float32. `decode` then casts the latents to
`post_quant_conv`'s dtype:

{{ code(PVAE, function='AutoencoderKLPH3Image.decode', root=DM) }}

Ours applies the same rule per class:

{{ code('src/h3mlx/load.py', var='VAE_KEEP_F32') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/load.py', function='vae_dtypes') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/load.py', function='load_vae') }}

The run script loads the image VAE in float32 by default, as h3mlx decodes H3; `--vae-dtype bfloat16` loads its
weights the pipeline's way. `test_vae_bf16_loading_matches_from_pretrained` compares every parameter's dtype with
`from_pretrained(torch_dtype=bfloat16)` for both classes.

That matches the weights, not every rounding of the activations. One known difference on the bf16 path: diffusers
rounds the final LayerNorm's output to bf16 (the norm runs in the decoder's dtype) and then widens it for the float32
`proj_out`; ours computes the norm in float32 and keeps that result into `proj_out` (`vit_decoder`'s last lines). On a
tiny model, adding that rounding moved the output by up to 5e-3 without lowering the error against torch, so it is
left as is. The default float32 decode rounds nothing.

The pixels are then un-normalised and clamped, and rounded to 8 bits the way `VaeImageProcessor.numpy_to_pil` rounds:

{{ code(P + 'decoders.py', function='PH3ImageDecodeStep.__call__', root=DM) }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/image.py', function='decode') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/image.py', function='to_uint8') }}

`test_image_decode_matches` (three latent sizes, tiled), `test_image_decode_untiled_and_frame_position` and
`test_image_decode_bf16_policy_matches_pipeline` compare the decoder with diffusers'.

## 9. The prompt upsampler

The upsampler is a Qwen3.5-9B fine-tune. It gets the request in a chat template, with the aspect ratio for
text-to-image or the pictures for an edit, and one greedy generation writes its reasoning, `</think>`, then the prompt.
An edit's answer must be a brief in six sections or a one-paragraph instruction that says what it keeps.

{{ code(P + 'prompt_upsampler.py', function='upsample_prompt', root=DM) }}
{{ code(P + 'prompt_upsampler.py', function='PH3ImageText2ImagePromptUpsampleStep.__call__', root=DM) }}

Ours builds the same request with the same processor and runs the model in mlx-vlm, whose `generate_step` decodes
greedily at temperature 0:

{{ code('src/h3mlx/upsampler.py', function='chat_text') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/upsampler.py', function='generate_ids') }}
{{ code('src/h3mlx/upsampler.py', function='upsample') }}

The text-to-image label is taken from the requested size before the canvas is rounded, as the pipeline does
(`run_p_h3_image.upsampler_aspect`). Greedy decoding in MLX and in torch can part ways once two logits are within
rounding of each other, so on the 9B model the prompt it writes can differ from torch's in places; the same validity
checks decide whether it is usable. The tests: `test_requests_and_answers_match_diffusers` runs diffusers'
`upsample_prompt` with a stand-in model that records its inputs and answers with fixed text, and compares input ids,
pixels, grids and the returned prompt; `test_answers_are_judged_as_diffusers_judges_them` and
`test_edit_answers_must_be_edit_prompts` feed both sides the same answers (cut off, no `</think>`, two of them, too
short, a text-to-image answer to an edit) and check they accept and refuse the same ones;
`test_tiny_prefill_logits_match_transformers` and
`test_tiny_greedy_tokens_match_transformers` compare a tiny random Qwen3.5 in transformers with the same weights in
mlx-vlm (with and without pictures). On the release, for "give her a wide straw hat", the upsampler generated 312
tokens (its reasoning, the brief and the end token) in 14 s, and the brief it wrote is valid and 224 tokens long.

## 10. End to end

{{ code('src/h3mlx/image.py', function='generate') }}

With `free_transformer`, `generate` also drops the connector once the prompt is encoded and, for an edit, the VAE
encoder once the pictures are (`VideoVAE.drop_encoder`). A text-to-image request never evaluates the encoder's lazily
loaded weights. `test_single_use_components_free_their_weights_and_change_nothing` runs an edit both ways and checks
the image is the same.

`test_end_to_end_matches_diffusers` runs diffusers' `PH3ImageBlocks` (upsampler removed) and `generate` on the same
tiny random components: text-to-image, an edit with two pictures at Picture 1's size, and one on a set canvas. It
compares the token tags exactly, the prompt embeddings within 1e-4 (absolute and relative), the noised picture rows,
and the decoded images to 2e-5 (measured: under 1e-6). `tests/test_run_p_h3_image.py` saves those components in the
release's own layout and runs the command line against `PH3ImageBlocks` for both workflows, plus the re-decode path
and its refusals.

**The modulation cache.** Each block's `adaln_proj` (2688 → 96,768, 13B parameters across the 50 blocks, 40% of the
transformer) sees only the timestep embedding, and a P-H3-Image run has about ten distinct timesteps: the 8 grid points,
plus 0.999 for an edit's pictures. `generate(modulation_cache=True)` builds the main guide's `ModulationCache` (§2.6)
for each of the 8 calls and, with `free_transformer`, drops each block's projection as soon as its tables are built
(the weights load lazily, so at most one is resident). The run script does this by default:

{{ code('src/h3mlx/image.py', marker='modulation-cache') }}

`test_modulation_cache_matches_diffusers_and_drops_adaln` runs both workflows cached against diffusers (1.8e-7) and
requires them bit-identical to our uncached path, and checks the projections are gone. That equality is new: the
first version batched all ten timesteps into one product, and on the release that drew a different picture from the
same seed (17.5 dB from the uncached image), while the per-call tables reproduce it exactly. Pruna's ComfyUI files make the same cut a different
way: a table of 4,097 timesteps by 16 numbers and 16-wide projections, a rank-16 fit of the same curve rather than its
exact values.

**Measured on an M3 Ultra (60-core GPU, 256 GB)** with the release, 8 calls, bf16 transformer, float32 decode. The
first two rows ran at commits `fcf379a` (text-to-image) and `ac3da69` (edit), before `generate` dropped the connector
and encoder after use; the last two at the commits shown. The total includes loading; the peak is MLX's active
allocations, in decimal GB:

| request | size | rows | warm call (mean after the first) | total | MLX peak (GB) |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| text-to-image, prompt as written | 2912 × 2912 | 8,395 | 26.0 s | 4.2 min | 69 GB |
| edit, one 8.5 MP picture, upsampled | 2912 × 2912 | 17,749 | 69.4 s | 10.5 min | 72 GB |
| edit, one 1.6 MP picture, upsampled (`96d5198`) | 2368 × 1760 | 9,382 | 29.8 s | 4.7 min | 69.3 GB |
| the same, with the modulation cache (`e3b94ef`) | 2368 × 1760 | 9,320 | 29.3 s | 4.6 min | 43.3 GB |

The cache's saving is the 26 GB of projections; the speed is unchanged (reading them cost about 30 ms a call).

## 11. Not done, and open

- **Real-weight equivalence with torch is not measured.** Every comparison above uses tiny random models; the release
  has only run on our side. A bf16 run of both pipelines on the same seed would show how far they drift.
- **Not ported:** diffusers' component offloading and tensor parallelism, and Pruna's ComfyUI files (int8, w4a8, and a
  20B transformer whose AdaLN projections are replaced by a table).
- **Pruna's rank-16 AdaLN table** is not ported; ours caches the exact values instead (§10).
